WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
TypeScript helps API security by making developer intent visible.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:18.120
Types can show the shape of request objects after validation, the fields a service expects, the return type of a policy check, and the difference between a public response model and an internal database record.

3
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.180
That clarity reduces accidental misuse.

4
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.260
TypeScript does not, by itself, validate data arriving over HTTP.

5
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:32.920
A request body, query value, path parameter, or header is runtime data from outside the program.

6
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.180
The compiler cannot prove that a JSON payload from a client matches an interface unless the application checks it at runtime.

7
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.720
This is why TypeScript types and validation schemas should work together.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.920
Strict compiler settings matter.

9
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:51.180
Options such as strict null checks and no implicit any help reveal uncertainty early.

10
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:58.240
The type unknown is often safer than any because it forces code to narrow a value before using it.

11
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:08.580
Discriminated unions can make expected states explicit, and schema-derived types can reduce drift between runtime validation and compile-time assumptions.

12
00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.620
Teams should be careful with type assertions that silence uncertainty.

13
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:19.760
An assertion can be useful at a trusted boundary, but frequent assertions around request data are a warning sign.

14
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:29.220
Secure TypeScript API design uses types to document safe states, validation to create those states, and review to prevent broad types from hiding important risk.

15
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.700
Good type design also improves collaboration.

16
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:41.360
When route handlers, service functions, and response mappers use narrow types, reviewers can see what has already been checked and what still needs protection.

17
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.020
This makes security expectations easier to pass from one engineer to another.
